const CARDS_OPHTHO_TRAUMA = [
{ q: "What does the BETT classification stand for and what are its two main categories?", a: "<b>Birmingham Eye Trauma Terminology</b>.<br>Two main categories:<br>1. <b>Open globe</b> — full-thickness wound of the eye wall (sclera and/or cornea)<br>2. <b>Closed globe</b> — no full-thickness wound of the eye wall" },
{ q: "Under BETT, how is open globe injury subdivided?", a: "<b>Open globe</b> subdivisions:<br>1. <b>Rupture</b> — caused by <i>blunt</i> force (inside-out mechanism, globe bursts at weakest point)<br>2. <b>Laceration</b> — caused by <i>sharp</i> object, subdivided into:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Penetrating</b> — single full-thickness entry wound<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Perforating</b> — entry <i>and</i> exit wound (through-and-through)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>IOFB</b> — intraocular foreign body retained inside the globe" },
{ q: "Under BETT, how is closed globe injury subdivided?", a: "<b>Closed globe</b> subdivisions:<br>1. <b>Contusion</b> — blunt force, no full-thickness wound (e.g., hyphema, angle recession)<br>2. <b>Lamellar laceration</b> — partial-thickness wound of the eye wall (sharp object does <i>not</i> penetrate fully)" },
{ q: "Distinguish penetrating vs perforating eye injury.", a: "<b>Penetrating</b> = single full-thickness entry wound only (object enters but does not exit).<br><b>Perforating</b> = entry wound <i>plus</i> exit wound (through-and-through injury).<br>Both are subtypes of open globe laceration (BETT)." },
{ q: "What is the single most important prognostic indicator in ocular trauma and when should it be documented?", a: "<b>Visual acuity</b> is the most important prognostic indicator.<br>It must be documented <b>FIRST</b>, before any examination or manipulation of the globe." },
{ q: "What does an APD (afferent pupillary defect) indicate in ocular trauma?", a: "An <b>APD (Marcus Gunn pupil)</b> indicates <b>significant posterior segment damage</b> — optic nerve injury, extensive retinal damage, or vitreous hemorrhage.<br>Tested with the <b>swinging flashlight test</b>." },
{ q: "Why should Goldmann tonometry and forced duction testing be avoided when open globe is suspected?", a: "Both apply <b>pressure to the globe</b>, which can cause further <b>extrusion of intraocular contents</b> through the wound.<br>• No Goldmann tonometry (use Tono-Pen gently if needed, or defer)<br>• No forced duction testing" },
{ q: "What is the proper eye protection for a suspected open globe?", a: "A <b>rigid Fox shield</b> (metal or plastic) taped over the brow and cheek.<br>• Do <b>NOT</b> use a pressure patch — this can push intraocular contents out through the wound.<br>• The shield must not contact the globe." },
{ q: "Why is MRI contraindicated in suspected metallic intraocular foreign body?", a: "MRI uses strong magnetic fields that can cause a <b>metallic foreign body to move</b> within the eye, resulting in further tissue damage.<br>The imaging modality of choice is <b>CT orbit</b> (non-contrast, axial and coronal thin cuts)." },
{ q: "What is the Ocular Trauma Score (OTS)?", a: "The <b>OTS</b> is a prognostic scoring system that predicts <b>visual outcome</b> after ocular trauma.<br>Variables include:<br>• Initial visual acuity (most heavily weighted)<br>• Globe rupture<br>• Endophthalmitis<br>• Perforating injury<br>• Retinal detachment<br>• Relative afferent pupillary defect<br>Higher score = better visual prognosis." },
{ q: "List the classic signs of open globe injury.", a: "1. <b>Irregular/peaked pupil</b> (pointing toward wound site)<br>2. <b>Shallow or deep AC</b> (depending on wound location)<br>3. <b>Positive Seidel test</b> (fluorescein streaming = aqueous leak)<br>4. <b>Exposed uveal tissue</b> (dark brown tissue in wound)<br>5. <b>360° subconjunctival hemorrhage</b><br>6. <b>Lens opacity or subluxation</b><br>7. <b>Vitreous hemorrhage</b><br>8. <b>Hypotony</b> (low IOP)" },
{ q: "What is the Seidel test and what does a positive result mean?", a: "A concentrated <b>fluorescein strip</b> is applied over the suspected wound site, observed with cobalt blue light.<br><b>Positive Seidel test</b> = a <i>stream of bright green aqueous humor</i> dilutes the dark fluorescein → indicates <b>full-thickness wound with aqueous leak</b> (open globe)." },
{ q: "Why does the pupil become peaked/irregular in an open globe injury?", a: "The pupil becomes <b>peaked and points toward the wound</b> because:<br>• <b>Iris tissue is drawn toward the site of corneal/scleral laceration</b> by the flow of aqueous humor out of the wound (iris plugs the wound).<br>• This creates an irregular, teardrop-shaped pupil." },
{ q: "What is the emergency management of open globe injury?", a: "1. <b>Rigid Fox shield</b> (no pressure patch)<br>2. <b>NPO</b> (nothing by mouth — anticipate surgery under GA)<br>3. <b>IV antibiotics</b>: vancomycin + fluoroquinolone or ceftazidime<br>4. <b>Tetanus prophylaxis</b><br>5. <b>NO topical medications/ointments</b> (can be toxic intraocularly)<br>6. <b>Emergent surgical repair</b><br>7. <b>Avoid Valsalva</b> (no coughing, straining, bending)<br>8. Antiemetics if needed" },
{ q: "Why are topical ointments avoided in open globe injury?", a: "Topical ointments can <b>enter the eye through the wound</b> and are <b>toxic to intraocular structures</b> (especially the retina).<br>Systemic (IV) antibiotics are used instead." },
{ q: "What is the most common eye injury overall?", a: "<b>Corneal abrasion</b> — a defect in the corneal epithelium, usually from trauma (fingernail, paper edge, contact lens)." },
{ q: "What are the symptoms and signs of corneal abrasion?", a: "<b>Symptoms</b>: foreign body sensation, tearing, photophobia, pain, blurred vision.<br><b>Signs</b>: <b>fluorescein staining</b> reveals the epithelial defect (bright green under cobalt blue light), conjunctival injection." },
{ q: "How is corneal abrasion treated?", a: "1. <b>Topical antibiotics</b>: erythromycin ointment or fluoroquinolone drops<br>2. <b>Cycloplegic</b> for pain relief (e.g., cyclopentolate 1%) — reduces ciliary spasm<br>3. Oral analgesics if needed<br>4. Healing expected in <b>24–48 hours</b><br>5. <b>NO patching</b> for contact lens wearers (risk of <i>Pseudomonas</i> infection)" },
{ q: "Why should the eye NOT be patched in a contact lens wearer with corneal abrasion?", a: "Contact lens wearers are at increased risk of <b><i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i></b> infection.<br>A pressure patch creates a <b>warm, dark, moist environment</b> that promotes bacterial growth. Instead, use <b>fluoroquinolone drops</b> with anti-pseudomonal coverage." },
{ q: "What is recurrent corneal erosion syndrome and its classic association?", a: "<b>Recurrent corneal erosion syndrome</b> = repeated spontaneous breakdown of corneal epithelium, typically <b>upon waking</b> (lids stick to loose epithelium during sleep).<br>Classic association: <b>Epithelial basement membrane dystrophy (map-dot-fingerprint dystrophy)</b>.<br>Also occurs after prior corneal abrasion." },
{ q: "How is a corneal foreign body managed?", a: "1. <b>Slit-lamp removal</b> with needle tip or spud under topical anesthesia<br>2. If iron FB → remove <b>rust ring with rotary burr</b> within 24 hours<br>3. Perform <b>Seidel test</b> to ensure no penetration (must be negative)<br>4. <b>Topical antibiotics</b> post-removal<br>5. Follow up in 24 hours" },
{ q: "What is a rust ring and why must it be removed?", a: "A <b>rust ring</b> forms around an iron/steel corneal foreign body from oxidation.<br>It must be removed (typically with a <b>sterile burr</b>) because:<br>• It acts as a <b>nidus for inflammation and infection</b><br>• It impairs epithelial healing<br>• Can cause permanent corneal scarring if left in place" },
{ q: "What characterizes a spontaneous subconjunctival hemorrhage?", a: "<b>Bright red, painless, well-demarcated</b> area of blood under conjunctiva.<br>• No vision change, no discharge<br>• <b>Self-resolving in 2–3 weeks</b><br>• Common causes: Valsalva, coughing, sneezing, anticoagulants, HTN<br>• Check <b>BP and bleeding diathesis</b> if recurrent" },
{ q: "When should subconjunctival hemorrhage raise concern for open globe?", a: "If the hemorrhage is <b>360°</b> (circumferential) or if you <b>cannot see its posterior limit</b>, suspect an underlying <b>open globe injury</b> or scleral rupture.<br>These patients need further evaluation (CT orbit, careful exam)." },
{ q: "What is a hyphema and what is its most common cause?", a: "<b>Hyphema</b> = blood in the <b>anterior chamber</b>.<br>Most common cause: <b>blunt trauma</b> → tears of the <b>iris root or ciliary body vessels</b>.<br>Seen as a layered red blood collection in the lower AC when patient is upright." },
{ q: "How is hyphema graded?", a: "<b>Hyphema grading</b>:<br>• <b>Grade I</b>: < 1/3 of AC filled with blood<br>• <b>Grade II</b>: 1/3 to 1/2 of AC<br>• <b>Grade III</b>: > 1/2 of AC<br>• <b>Grade IV</b>: Total hyphema (<b>8-ball hyphema</b>) — entire AC filled, appears <b>black/dark red</b>" },
{ q: "What is the management of traumatic hyphema?", a: "1. <b>Rigid eye shield</b><br>2. <b>Bed rest with head elevated 30–45°</b> (allows blood to settle inferiorly)<br>3. <b>Topical cycloplegic</b> (atropine) — reduces pain and prevents posterior synechiae<br>4. <b>Topical steroid</b> — reduces inflammation<br>5. <b>Topical timolol</b> — controls IOP<br>6. <b>NO aspirin/NSAIDs</b> (increases bleeding risk)<br>7. Daily IOP checks" },
{ q: "When does rebleeding typically occur after traumatic hyphema?", a: "Rebleeding peaks at <b>day 3–5</b> after injury.<br>Rebleeding is often <b>worse than the initial bleed</b> and is associated with increased complications (glaucoma, corneal blood staining)." },
{ q: "What are the major complications of hyphema?", a: "1. <b>Rebleeding</b> (peaks day 3–5)<br>2. <b>Secondary glaucoma</b> (from RBCs clogging trabecular meshwork)<br>3. <b>Corneal blood staining</b> (hemoglobin-stained cornea — may take years to clear)<br>4. <b>Optic atrophy</b> from sustained elevated IOP<br>5. <b>Posterior synechiae</b>" },
{ q: "Why are sickle cell patients at special risk with hyphema?", a: "In sickle cell disease (including trait):<br>• <b>Sickled RBCs obstruct trabecular meshwork more readily</b> → IOP rises faster<br>• The <b>low-oxygen, acidotic AC environment</b> promotes further sickling<br>• Even mildly elevated IOP can cause <b>optic nerve damage</b> (compromised optic nerve blood supply)" },
{ q: "Which medications must be AVOIDED in sickle cell patients with hyphema?", a: "1. <b>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</b> (acetazolamide) — lowers AC pH, <b>worsens sickling</b> in the anterior chamber<br>2. <b>Epinephrine</b> — can cause vasoconstriction and worsen sickling<br>These patients have a <b>lower threshold for surgical evacuation</b> of the hyphema." },
{ q: "What is the typical presentation of traumatic iritis?", a: "<b>Traumatic iritis</b>:<br>• <b>Photophobia</b> and pain<br>• <b>Ciliary flush</b> (perilimbal injection)<br>• <b>Cells and flare</b> in the anterior chamber (on slit lamp)<br>• <b>Miotic pupil</b> (from iris sphincter spasm)<br>• History of blunt eye trauma" },
{ q: "How is traumatic iritis treated?", a: "1. <b>Topical cycloplegic</b> (e.g., cyclopentolate or homatropine) — relieves ciliary spasm and prevents posterior synechiae<br>2. <b>Topical corticosteroid</b> (e.g., prednisolone acetate) — reduces inflammation" },
{ q: "What is traumatic mydriasis and why may it be permanent?", a: "<b>Traumatic mydriasis</b> = dilated pupil after blunt trauma due to <b>tears in the iris sphincter muscle</b>.<br>It may be <b>permanent</b> because the sphincter muscle fibers are physically disrupted and do not regenerate.<br>The pupil is often <b>irregularly dilated</b> with visible sphincter tears on slit-lamp exam." },
{ q: "What is angle recession glaucoma and what causes it?", a: "<b>Angle recession glaucoma</b> results from blunt trauma causing a <b>tear in the ciliary body</b> — specifically between the <b>longitudinal and circular muscle fibers</b>.<br>On gonioscopy: <b>widened ciliary body band</b> (abnormally wide angle).<br>Mechanism: <b>open-angle glaucoma</b> from damaged trabecular meshwork." },
{ q: "What is the timeline for angle recession glaucoma and how should it be monitored?", a: "Can present <b>months to years after the initial trauma</b>.<br>Patients require <b>lifelong IOP monitoring</b> — at least annual checks with gonioscopy.<br>Rule: Any patient with history of significant blunt eye trauma should be monitored for angle recession glaucoma indefinitely." },
{ q: "What is a Vossius ring?", a: "A <b>Vossius ring</b> is a <b>ring of iris pigment on the anterior lens capsule</b>, caused by blunt trauma.<br>It results from the <b>pupillary margin of the iris being imprinted onto the lens</b> by the concussive force.<br>It is a sign of <b>significant blunt trauma</b> but does not impair vision." },
{ q: "What is the characteristic pattern of a traumatic cataract?", a: "A <b>rosette-shaped (flower-petal) cataract</b> — feathery opacities radiating from the center of the lens, often seen in the posterior subcapsular region.<br>Can develop <b>acutely or years after trauma</b>." },
{ q: "What signs suggest lens subluxation or dislocation after trauma?", a: "1. <b>Iridodonesis</b> — trembling/quivering of the iris (loss of lens support)<br>2. <b>Phacodonesis</b> — trembling of the lens<br>3. <b>Monocular diplopia</b> (edge of subluxated lens in visual axis)<br>4. <b>Unequal AC depth</b><br>5. <b>Visible lens equator</b> on dilated exam" },
{ q: "What secondary glaucoma types can occur from lens injury?", a: "1. <b>Phacomorphic glaucoma</b> — swollen/intumescent lens pushes iris forward → <b>angle closure</b> (pupillary block)<br>2. <b>Phacolytic glaucoma</b> — leakage of <b>lens proteins through intact capsule</b> of a hypermature cataract → macrophages clog trabecular meshwork → <b>open-angle glaucoma</b>" },
{ q: "What is the mechanism of an orbital floor blowout fracture?", a: "Blunt trauma to the orbit → sudden increase in <b>intraorbital pressure</b> (hydraulic mechanism) → fracture at the <b>weakest wall</b> of the orbit.<br>Most common: <b>orbital floor</b> (maxillary bone) > <b>medial wall</b> (lamina papyracea)." },
{ q: "What are the classic clinical findings of orbital floor blowout fracture?", a: "1. <b>Restricted upgaze</b> (entrapment of inferior rectus/orbital tissue)<br>2. <b>Enophthalmos</b> (sunken eye — from increased orbital volume)<br>3. <b>Infraorbital hypoesthesia</b> (V2 — numbness of cheek/upper lip/upper teeth)<br>4. <b>Diplopia</b> (especially in upgaze)<br>5. <b>Subcutaneous emphysema</b> (crepitus — air from maxillary sinus)<br>6. Periorbital ecchymosis and edema" },
{ q: "What is the teardrop sign on CT in orbital floor fracture?", a: "The <b>teardrop sign</b> = a <b>teardrop-shaped soft tissue density</b> hanging into the maxillary sinus on coronal CT.<br>It represents <b>herniated orbital fat and/or inferior rectus muscle</b> through the floor fracture." },
{ q: "What is a white-eyed blowout fracture and why is it an emergency?", a: "A <b>white-eyed blowout fracture</b> occurs in <b>children</b> — minimal external bruising (\"white eye\") but <b>tight entrapment of inferior rectus</b> in a greenstick fracture.<br>It is an <b>emergency</b> because:<br>• Causes <b>oculocardiac reflex</b> → <b>bradycardia, nausea, vomiting</b><br>• Can lead to <b>muscle ischemia and permanent diplopia</b><br>• Requires <b>emergency surgical release</b>" },
{ q: "What are the indications for surgical repair of orbital blowout fracture?", a: "1. <b>Persistent diplopia in primary gaze</b> after 2 weeks (not resolving with swelling reduction)<br>2. <b>Enophthalmos > 2 mm</b><br>3. <b>Large floor defect (> 50%)</b> of orbital floor<br>4. <b>Muscle entrapment</b> (especially in children — emergent)<br>Otherwise, wait <b>1–2 weeks</b> for swelling to resolve before deciding." },
{ q: "What is orbital compartment syndrome?", a: "<b>Orbital compartment syndrome</b> = rapidly rising orbital pressure (usually from <b>retrobulbar hemorrhage</b>) that compresses the <b>optic nerve and central retinal artery</b>, causing <b>acute vision loss</b>.<br>This is an <b>ophthalmic emergency</b>." },
{ q: "What are the signs of orbital compartment syndrome?", a: "1. <b>Proptosis</b> (eye pushed forward)<br>2. <b>Decreased vision</b> (rapid onset)<br>3. <b>APD</b> (afferent pupillary defect)<br>4. <b>Elevated IOP</b> (rock-hard globe)<br>5. <b>Restricted/frozen extraocular motility</b><br>6. <b>Tense, firm orbit</b> on palpation<br>7. Periorbital swelling and ecchymosis" },
{ q: "What is the emergency treatment for orbital compartment syndrome?", a: "<b>Lateral canthotomy and inferior cantholysis</b>:<br>1. Clamp the lateral canthus with a hemostat<br>2. Cut the lateral canthal tendon<br>3. <b>Release the inferior crus</b> of the lateral canthal tendon (cantholysis)<br>This decompresses the orbit by allowing the globe to move forward.<br><b>Must be done within minutes</b> — can be performed at the bedside by any trained physician." },
{ q: "What are the two types of traumatic optic neuropathy?", a: "1. <b>Direct</b> — bone fragment or foreign body directly <b>lacerates</b> the optic nerve<br>2. <b>Indirect</b> (most common) — <b>contrecoup/transmitted force</b> damages the optic nerve without direct penetration (e.g., deceleration injury, forehead blow)" },
{ q: "What are the clinical findings in traumatic optic neuropathy?", a: "1. <b>Decreased visual acuity</b> (can range from mild to NLP)<br>2. <b>APD</b> (afferent pupillary defect — hallmark)<br>3. <b>Normal fundus initially</b> — disc pallor develops <b>weeks later</b> as optic atrophy ensues<br>4. Visual field defects<br>5. Decreased color vision" },
{ q: "What is the current treatment approach for traumatic optic neuropathy?", a: "Treatment is <b>controversial</b>. Options include:<br>1. <b>Observation</b> alone<br>2. <b>High-dose IV methylprednisolone</b> (previously used, but <b>CRASH trial</b> raised concerns about increased mortality with high-dose steroids in trauma)<br>3. <b>Optic canal decompression surgery</b> (if CT shows bone impinging on optic canal)<br>Currently, no treatment has strong evidence of superiority." },
{ q: "Where are the most common sites of globe rupture in adults?", a: "1. <b>Limbus</b> (thinnest area in adults — junction of cornea and sclera)<br>2. At the <b>insertion of rectus muscles</b> (thin sclera at muscle attachment)<br>3. At <b>previous surgical incision/wound sites</b> (e.g., old cataract or glaucoma surgery sites)" },
{ q: "Distinguish the mechanism of rupture vs laceration in open globe injury.", a: "<b>Rupture</b> (blunt force):<br>• Inside-out mechanism — IOP spike bursts the globe at the <b>weakest point</b><br>• Wound is usually <b>away from impact site</b><br><br><b>Laceration</b> (sharp force):<br>• Outside-in mechanism — sharp object cuts through<br>• Wound is <b>at the impact site</b>" },
{ q: "What are the signs of globe rupture?", a: "1. <b>Extrusion of intraocular contents</b> (uveal tissue, vitreous, lens)<br>2. <b>Flat anterior chamber</b> (collapsed)<br>3. <b>Hypotony</b> (very low IOP)<br>4. <b>Irregular/peaked pupil</b><br>5. <b>360° subconjunctival hemorrhage</b><br>6. <b>Decreased vision</b> (often severe)<br>7. <b>Positive Seidel test</b>" },
{ q: "What is siderosis bulbi?", a: "<b>Siderosis bulbi</b> = toxicity from a retained <b>iron intraocular foreign body</b>.<br>Iron dissociates and deposits in ocular tissues causing:<br>• <b>Brown discoloration</b> of iris (heterochromia)<br>• <b>Cataract</b><br>• <b>Retinal degeneration</b><br>• <b>Decreased ERG amplitudes</b> (b-wave reduction — early sign)<br>• Secondary glaucoma" },
{ q: "What is chalcosis?", a: "<b>Chalcosis</b> = toxicity from a retained <b>copper intraocular foreign body</b>.<br>Features:<br>• <b>Sunflower cataract</b> (green-brown petal-shaped deposits on anterior lens capsule)<br>• <b>Kayser-Fleischer–like ring</b> (copper in Descemet's membrane)<br>• Retinal toxicity<br>If copper content is <b>> 85% pure</b> → <b>suppurative endophthalmitis</b> (severe inflammation, sterile)." },
{ q: "Which intraocular foreign body materials are generally well-tolerated?", a: "<b>Inert materials</b> that are generally tolerated:<br>• <b>Glass</b><br>• <b>Stone</b><br>• <b>Plastic</b><br>• <b>Gold, silver, platinum</b><br>These may sometimes be observed if small and in a non-critical location, but most IOFBs are surgically removed to prevent endophthalmitis." },
{ q: "How is IOFB managed surgically?", a: "<b>IOFB management</b>:<br>1. <b>CT orbit</b> to locate and characterize FB (NOT MRI if metallic)<br>2. <b>IV antibiotics</b> (high risk of endophthalmitis)<br>3. <b>Pars plana vitrectomy</b> + foreign body extraction (with forceps or magnet for ferrous FB)<br>4. <b>Endolaser</b> (prophylactic retinal treatment around entry site)<br>5. ± Lensectomy if lens damaged<br>6. Tetanus prophylaxis" },
{ q: "Why is alkali chemical burn worse than acid?", a: "<b>Alkali</b> causes <b>liquefactive necrosis</b> — it saponifies cell membranes and <b>penetrates rapidly and deeply</b> into ocular tissues, reaching the anterior chamber.<br><b>Acid</b> causes <b>coagulative necrosis</b> — protein denaturation creates a <b>barrier that limits further penetration</b>.<br>Exception: <b>hydrofluoric (HF) acid</b> penetrates like alkali." },
{ q: "Name common alkali and acid agents in chemical eye burns.", a: "<b>Alkali agents</b> (worse prognosis):<br>• <b>Lye</b> (NaOH — drain cleaners)<br>• <b>Ammonia</b> (cleaning products)<br>• <b>Lime/calcium hydroxide</b> (cement, plaster)<br><br><b>Acid agents</b>:<br>• Sulfuric acid (car batteries)<br>• Hydrochloric acid<br>• Acetic acid<br>• <b>Hydrofluoric acid</b> (penetrates like alkali — exception)" },
{ q: "What is the FIRST and most critical step in chemical eye burn management?", a: "<b>IMMEDIATE copious irrigation</b> — this takes priority over <i>everything</i> (even visual acuity).<br>• Use <b>saline, Ringer's lactate, or water</b> — whatever is immediately available<br>• Irrigate for at least <b>30 minutes</b><br>• <b>Check pH</b> — target <b>7.0–7.4</b><br>• Continue irrigation until pH is neutral<br>• Remove any particulate matter (especially lime particles in fornices)" },
{ q: "Describe the Roper-Hall classification for chemical burns.", a: "<b>Roper-Hall classification</b> (based on corneal clarity and limbal ischemia):<br>• <b>Grade I</b>: Clear cornea, no limbal ischemia → excellent prognosis<br>• <b>Grade II</b>: Hazy cornea (iris details visible), < 1/3 limbal ischemia → good prognosis<br>• <b>Grade III</b>: Total corneal epithelial loss, stromal haze (iris details obscured), 1/3–1/2 limbal ischemia → guarded prognosis<br>• <b>Grade IV</b>: Opaque cornea (no iris view), > 1/2 limbal ischemia → poor prognosis" },
{ q: "What is the medical treatment for chemical eye burns after irrigation?", a: "1. <b>Topical antibiotics</b> (prevent secondary infection)<br>2. <b>Cycloplegic</b> (pain relief, prevent synechiae)<br>3. <b>Topical steroids</b> — <i>first 7–10 days only</i> (after that, steroids inhibit collagen repair → increased perforation risk)<br>4. <b>Ascorbic acid</b> (oral and topical — promotes collagen synthesis)<br>5. <b>Citric acid/citrate drops</b> (inhibits PMN-mediated collagen breakdown)<br>6. <b>Amniotic membrane transplant</b> for severe burns" },
{ q: "What are the long-term complications of severe chemical eye burns?", a: "1. <b>Corneal scarring and opacification</b><br>2. <b>Corneal perforation</b> (from collagenolysis)<br>3. <b>Limbal stem cell deficiency</b> (conjunctivalization of cornea)<br>4. <b>Symblepharon</b> (adhesion of palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva)<br>5. <b>Secondary glaucoma</b><br>6. <b>Dry eye</b> (goblet cell destruction)<br>7. <b>Eyelid cicatricial changes</b>" },
{ q: "What is UV keratitis (welding flash/snow blindness)?", a: "<b>UV keratitis</b> = corneal epithelial damage from ultraviolet radiation.<br>Causes: welding without eye protection, tanning beds, snow reflection, high-altitude sun.<br>Features:<br>• <b>Delayed onset 6–12 hours</b> after exposure<br>• <b>Bilateral</b> punctate epithelial erosions<br>• <b>Severe pain</b>, foreign body sensation, tearing, photophobia<br>• <b>Self-resolving in 24–48 hours</b>" },
{ q: "How is UV keratitis treated?", a: "1. <b>Topical antibiotics</b> (prevent secondary infection)<br>2. <b>Cycloplegic</b> (pain relief)<br>3. <b>Oral analgesics</b><br>4. Cool compresses<br>5. <b>Self-resolving in 24–48 hours</b> — reassurance<br>No patching necessary." },
{ q: "What is solar retinopathy?", a: "<b>Solar retinopathy</b> = photic injury to the foveal retina from directly viewing a solar eclipse without adequate protection.<br>• Causes <b>RPE disruption and photoreceptor damage</b> at the fovea<br>• Central scotoma, metamorphopsia<br>• OCT: disruption of the outer retinal layers at fovea<br>• Usually <b>self-limited</b> with partial visual recovery" },
{ q: "What is the first priority when evaluating an eyelid laceration?", a: "<b>Rule out open globe injury first</b> — before any eyelid repair.<br>Any full-thickness eyelid laceration, especially from sharp trauma, has a significant association with underlying globe injury." },
{ q: "How are marginal (full-thickness) eyelid lacerations managed?", a: "<b>Marginal lacerations</b> (through the lid margin) require <b>meticulous layered repair by an ophthalmologist/oculoplastic surgeon</b>.<br>Layers to repair:<br>1. Posterior lamella (tarsus + conjunctiva)<br>2. Lid margin (precise alignment of gray line/lash line)<br>3. Anterior lamella (skin + orbicularis muscle)<br>Poor alignment → <b>lid notching, trichiasis, and corneal exposure</b>." },
{ q: "Why must medial eyelid lacerations be carefully evaluated?", a: "Medial eyelid lacerations may involve the <b>canalicular system</b> (lacrimal drainage).<br>If canalicular injury is suspected → repair with <b>silicone stent placement</b> to maintain the drainage pathway.<br>Failure to repair → chronic <b>epiphora</b> (tearing)." },
{ q: "Why must levator muscle involvement be identified in upper eyelid lacerations?", a: "The <b>levator palpebrae superioris</b> elevates the upper lid.<br>If lacerated and not repaired → <b>traumatic ptosis</b> (drooping of the upper eyelid).<br>Must identify and repair the levator aponeurosis during wound closure." },
{ q: "What is commotio retinae (Berlin edema)?", a: "<b>Commotio retinae</b> = whitening of the outer retina from <b>photoreceptor disruption</b> after blunt trauma (contrecoup injury).<br>• <b>Grayish-white retinal opacification</b>, often in the posterior pole<br>• If involving the macula → decreased central vision<br>• Usually <b>self-resolving</b> over weeks, but can leave permanent RPE changes if severe" },
{ q: "What is a traumatic choroidal rupture?", a: "<b>Choroidal rupture</b> = a <b>crescent-shaped pale streak</b> in the fundus, typically <b>concentric to the optic disc</b>.<br>• Caused by blunt trauma (contrecoup mechanism)<br>• Associated with <b>subretinal hemorrhage</b> acutely<br>• If through the macula → permanent <b>central vision loss</b><br>• Late complication: <b>choroidal neovascular membrane (CNVM)</b>" },
{ q: "What is retinal dialysis and who is most commonly affected?", a: "<b>Retinal dialysis</b> = a <b>tear at the ora serrata</b> (junction of retina and pars plana).<br>• Most common in <b>young males after blunt trauma</b><br>• Most common location: <b>inferotemporal quadrant</b> (superonasal in non-traumatic)<br>• Can lead to <b>rhegmatogenous retinal detachment</b><br>• Often slowly progressive — may present weeks-months after trauma" },
{ q: "What is sympathetic ophthalmia?", a: "<b>Sympathetic ophthalmia</b> = <b>bilateral granulomatous panuveitis</b> occurring <b>weeks to months</b> after penetrating trauma or intraocular surgery to one eye.<br>• <b>Exciting eye</b> = injured eye<br>• <b>Sympathizing eye</b> = fellow (uninvolved) eye<br>• Autoimmune reaction against sequestered uveal antigens<br>• <b>Rare but devastating</b>" },
{ q: "What histological finding is characteristic of sympathetic ophthalmia?", a: "<b>Dalen-Fuchs nodules</b> = collections of <b>epithelioid cells between Bruch's membrane and the RPE</b>.<br>Histology shows <b>diffuse granulomatous inflammation</b> with lymphocytes, epithelioid cells, and giant cells, sparing the choriocapillaris." },
{ q: "How is sympathetic ophthalmia treated and prevented?", a: "<b>Treatment</b>:<br>• <b>High-dose systemic corticosteroids</b><br>• <b>Immunosuppressive agents</b> (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, cyclosporine) for steroid-sparing<br><br><b>Prevention</b> (controversial):<br>• <b>Enucleation of the exciting eye within 10–14 days</b> of injury (before sympathetic ophthalmia develops)<br>• Once sympathetic ophthalmia is established, enucleation is of <b>no benefit</b>" },
{ q: "What grading system uses AC blood level relative to total AC volume?", a: "The <b>hyphema grading system</b>:<br>• Grade I: < 1/3 AC<br>• Grade II: 1/3–1/2 AC<br>• Grade III: > 1/2 AC<br>• Grade IV: 100% (total / 8-ball hyphema)" },
{ q: "Why is head elevation at 30–45° important in hyphema management?", a: "Head elevation allows <b>blood to settle inferiorly</b> by gravity, which:<br>1. Keeps blood <b>away from the visual axis</b><br>2. Facilitates <b>absorption through the trabecular meshwork</b><br>3. Reduces risk of <b>corneal blood staining</b><br>4. Helps monitor for rebleeding" },
{ q: "What is an 8-ball hyphema and why is it significant?", a: "An <b>8-ball hyphema</b> = <b>Grade IV total hyphema</b> where the entire AC is filled with <b>dark, deoxygenated blood</b> (appears black).<br>It is significant because:<br>• Highest risk of <b>corneal blood staining</b><br>• Greatest risk of <b>secondary glaucoma</b><br>• Most likely to require <b>surgical evacuation</b> (AC washout)" },
{ q: "What is corneal blood staining and when does it occur?", a: "<b>Corneal blood staining</b> = deposition of <b>hemoglobin and hemosiderin in the corneal stroma</b>, giving a yellowish-brown discoloration.<br>Occurs with:<br>• <b>Prolonged/large hyphema</b> (especially total hyphema)<br>• <b>Elevated IOP</b><br>• <b>Compromised endothelium</b><br>May take <b>months to years to clear</b>, or may be permanent." },
{ q: "A patient with sickle cell trait presents with hyphema and elevated IOP. Why is acetazolamide contraindicated?", a: "<b>Acetazolamide</b> (a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor) is contraindicated because it:<br>1. <b>Decreases aqueous humor pH</b> (more acidic AC)<br>2. <b>Increases ascorbic acid concentration</b> in AC<br>3. Both promote <b>sickling of red blood cells</b> in the anterior chamber<br>4. Sickled cells further obstruct outflow → <b>worsening IOP elevation</b><br>Use <b>topical timolol</b> or surgical evacuation instead." },
{ q: "What gonioscopic finding is seen in angle recession?", a: "On gonioscopy: an <b>abnormally widened ciliary body band</b> (the space between the scleral spur and the iris root is broader than normal).<br>This represents a <b>tear between the longitudinal and circular fibers of the ciliary muscle</b>.<br>Compare with the <b>fellow eye</b> — asymmetry is diagnostic." },
{ q: "A patient presents with a peaked pupil, shallow AC, and positive Seidel test. What is the diagnosis?", a: "<b>Open globe injury</b> (likely corneal laceration).<br>Key findings:<br>• <b>Peaked pupil</b> — iris drawn toward wound<br>• <b>Shallow AC</b> — aqueous leak from anterior wound<br>• <b>Positive Seidel test</b> — confirming aqueous leak<br>Management: shield, NPO, IV antibiotics, emergent surgical repair." },
{ q: "What is the oculocardiac reflex and when is it clinically important in trauma?", a: "The <b>oculocardiac reflex</b> is a <b>trigemino-vagal reflex</b>:<br>• Afferent: <b>trigeminal nerve (V1)</b> — triggered by pressure/traction on extraocular muscles or globe<br>• Efferent: <b>vagus nerve (X)</b> → <b>bradycardia</b>, nausea, vomiting<br>Clinically important in <b>white-eyed blowout fractures</b> (children) where tight muscle entrapment triggers reflex → <b>emergency surgical release</b>." },
{ q: "What is the most common site of retinal dialysis after blunt trauma?", a: "The <b>inferotemporal quadrant</b> is the most common location for traumatic retinal dialysis.<br>(In contrast, non-traumatic dialysis tends to occur <b>superonasally</b>.)" },
{ q: "How does phacomorphic glaucoma differ from phacolytic glaucoma?", a: "<b>Phacomorphic glaucoma</b>:<br>• Swollen (intumescent) lens → pushes iris forward → <b>angle closure</b> (pupillary block)<br>• Lens capsule is <b>intact</b><br>• AC is <b>shallow</b><br><br><b>Phacolytic glaucoma</b>:<br>• Lens proteins leak through <b>intact capsule</b> of <b>hypermature/mature cataract</b><br>• Macrophages engulf proteins → clog TM → <b>open-angle</b> glaucoma<br>• AC may have macrophage-laden flare" },
{ q: "What findings differentiate an anterior (corneal) wound vs posterior (scleral) wound in open globe injury?", a: "<b>Anterior wound (corneal)</b>:<br>• <b>Shallow AC</b> (aqueous leaking out)<br>• Peaked pupil toward corneal wound<br>• Visible corneal wound<br><br><b>Posterior wound (scleral)</b>:<br>• <b>Deep AC</b> (vitreous loss posteriorly)<br>• Vitreous hemorrhage<br>• May have <b>360° subconjunctival hemorrhage</b><br>• Wound often not directly visible" },
{ q: "What imaging is used for suspected orbital fractures?", a: "<b>CT orbit</b> (non-contrast, thin-cut axial and coronal views) is the gold standard.<br>• Best for identifying bony fractures, herniated tissue, and foreign bodies<br>• Look for: teardrop sign, air in orbit, fracture lines<br>• <b>Avoid MRI</b> if metallic foreign body is suspected<br>• Plain X-rays have low sensitivity (rarely used)" },
{ q: "Why does enophthalmos occur after orbital floor fracture?", a: "<b>Enophthalmos</b> (sunken eye) occurs because:<br>• Fracture of the orbital floor <b>increases orbital volume</b> (tissue herniates into maxillary sinus)<br>• The globe <b>sinks posteriorly</b> into the enlarged orbit<br>• May be masked initially by orbital edema (becomes apparent as swelling resolves)" },
{ q: "What nerve is damaged in orbital floor fracture and what are its symptoms?", a: "The <b>infraorbital nerve (V2)</b> runs along the orbital floor and is commonly injured.<br>Results in <b>hypoesthesia/numbness</b> of:<br>• <b>Ipsilateral cheek</b><br>• <b>Upper lip</b><br>• <b>Upper teeth/gums</b><br>• <b>Side of nose</b><br>Numbness may be temporary or permanent." },
{ q: "What is the significance of subcutaneous emphysema (crepitus) after orbital trauma?", a: "<b>Subcutaneous emphysema</b> = air tracking from a <b>paranasal sinus</b> (usually maxillary or ethmoid) through a <b>fracture site</b> into the orbital/periorbital soft tissues.<br>Indicates an <b>orbital wall fracture</b> communicating with a sinus.<br>Instruct patient to <b>avoid nose blowing</b> (can force air into orbit → orbital emphysema or compartment syndrome)." },
{ q: "In orbital compartment syndrome, why must lateral canthotomy be performed emergently?", a: "The optic nerve and <b>central retinal artery</b> are compressed by rising orbital pressure.<br><b>Irreversible vision loss</b> can occur within <b>60–90 minutes</b> without decompression.<br>Lateral canthotomy + cantholysis is the fastest way to decompress the orbit, can be done at bedside, and should <b>not wait for imaging or ophthalmology consultation</b>." },
{ q: "What is the difference between canthotomy and cantholysis?", a: "<b>Canthotomy</b> = cutting the lateral canthus (the junction of upper and lower lids at the outer corner).<br><b>Cantholysis</b> = cutting the <b>inferior crus of the lateral canthal tendon</b> to release the lower lid from the orbital rim.<br>Both together decompress the orbit by allowing the globe and orbital contents to move anteriorly." },
{ q: "What ERG finding is an early indicator of siderosis bulbi?", a: "<b>Decreased b-wave amplitude</b> on electroretinography (ERG).<br>This is often the <b>earliest objective sign</b> of iron toxicity to the retina, even before clinical findings are apparent.<br>Progressive decrease → eventual extinguished ERG if FB not removed." },
{ q: "A copper IOFB with > 85% purity causes what severe reaction?", a: "<b>Suppurative endophthalmitis</b> — a severe, sterile inflammatory reaction that mimics infectious endophthalmitis.<br>This occurs with <b>high-purity (> 85%) copper</b> foreign bodies.<br>Lower-purity copper alloys (e.g., brass, bronze) cause <b>chalcosis</b> (chronic, less severe reaction)." },
{ q: "Why are topical steroids limited to the first 7–10 days in chemical burn treatment?", a: "After the first 7–10 days, topical steroids:<br>• <b>Inhibit collagen synthesis</b> by stromal keratocytes<br>• <b>Impair re-epithelialization</b><br>• Increase risk of <b>corneal melting and perforation</b> (stromal thinning)<br>They are beneficial early to control inflammation but harmful in the reparative phase." },
{ q: "What role does ascorbic acid play in chemical burn treatment?", a: "<b>Ascorbic acid (vitamin C)</b> — both topical and oral:<br>• <b>Promotes collagen synthesis</b> by corneal stromal fibroblasts<br>• Reduces the risk of <b>corneal perforation</b><br>• Acts as an antioxidant<br>It is a key adjunctive therapy, especially in <b>moderate-to-severe alkali burns</b>." },
{ q: "What is limbal stem cell deficiency and how does it relate to chemical burns?", a: "<b>Limbal stem cells</b> (at the corneoscleral junction) are responsible for <b>corneal epithelial renewal</b>.<br>Severe chemical burns destroy these cells → <b>limbal stem cell deficiency</b>:<br>• Conjunctival epithelium grows over the cornea (<b>conjunctivalization</b>)<br>• <b>Persistent epithelial defects</b><br>• <b>Corneal neovascularization</b><br>• <b>Chronic inflammation and opacification</b><br>Treatment: limbal stem cell transplant." },
{ q: "What is symblepharon?", a: "<b>Symblepharon</b> = <b>adhesion</b> between the palpebral (eyelid) conjunctiva and the bulbar (globe) conjunctiva.<br>Caused by severe conjunctival inflammation/destruction (chemical burns, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, cicatricial pemphigoid).<br>Results in <b>restricted eye movement, fornix shortening, and entropion</b>." },
{ q: "What variables comprise the Ocular Trauma Score (OTS)?", a: "<b>OTS calculation</b>:<br>Start with initial VA (raw points):<br>• NLP = 60, LP/HM = 70, 1/200–19/200 = 80, 20/200–20/50 = 90, ≥ 20/40 = 100<br><br>Subtract for:<br>• Globe rupture: −23<br>• Endophthalmitis: −17<br>• Perforating injury: −14<br>• Retinal detachment: −11<br>• APD: −10<br><br>Higher final score → better visual prognosis." },
{ q: "What is the most important factor in the OTS?", a: "<b>Initial visual acuity</b> is the most heavily weighted factor in the OTS and the single most important prognostic indicator for visual outcome after ocular trauma." },
{ q: "What type of retinal detachment occurs after trauma with retinal dialysis?", a: "<b>Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment</b> — caused by a break (dialysis) in the retina that allows <b>vitreous fluid to pass through</b> and separate the neurosensory retina from the RPE." },
{ q: "What is a giant retinal tear and its traumatic significance?", a: "A <b>giant retinal tear</b> = a retinal break extending <b>≥ 90° (3 clock hours)</b> of the circumference, usually at or near the ora serrata.<br>Can occur after severe blunt trauma.<br>Requires <b>vitrectomy with silicone oil or gas tamponade</b> for repair.<br>High risk of <b>PVR</b> (proliferative vitreoretinopathy)." },
{ q: "In blunt trauma, where does globe rupture typically occur relative to the impact site?", a: "In blunt trauma, globe rupture occurs <b>away from the impact site</b>, at the <b>weakest point</b> of the globe (limbus, muscle insertions, prior surgical sites).<br>This is because the <b>IOP spikes and the globe bursts at the point of least resistance</b> (inside-out mechanism)." },
{ q: "How do you determine if a corneal foreign body has penetrated the full thickness?", a: "Perform a <b>Seidel test</b>:<br>• Apply concentrated fluorescein over the wound site<br>• Observe under cobalt blue light<br>• <b>Negative Seidel</b> = no aqueous leak → partial-thickness (safe to remove at slit lamp)<br>• <b>Positive Seidel</b> = aqueous streaming → full-thickness wound (open globe) → emergent surgical management" },
{ q: "What antibiotic regimen is used for open globe injury?", a: "<b>IV antibiotics</b> for open globe:<br>• <b>Vancomycin</b> (covers gram-positive, including MRSA) <b>+</b><br>• <b>Fluoroquinolone</b> (e.g., levofloxacin) or <b>ceftazidime</b> (covers gram-negatives, especially <i>Pseudomonas</i>)<br>Goal: prevent <b>post-traumatic endophthalmitis</b>" },
{ q: "Why should patients with open globe injuries avoid Valsalva maneuvers?", a: "Valsalva maneuvers (coughing, straining, bearing down) <b>increase intraocular and venous pressure</b>, which can:<br>• Force <b>intraocular contents out through the wound</b><br>• Worsen hemorrhage<br>• Convert a contained injury into extrusion of uvea/vitreous<br>Prescribe antiemetics and stool softeners prophylactically." },
{ q: "What is the role of tetanus prophylaxis in ocular trauma?", a: "<b>Tetanus prophylaxis</b> should be administered for any <b>open globe injury or penetrating wound</b> — especially if the patient's immunization is not up to date or involves a contaminated/soil-exposed foreign body.<br>• Td booster if last vaccine > 5 years ago<br>• TIG (tetanus immunoglobulin) if not previously vaccinated" },
{ q: "What is the mechanism of the infraorbital nerve injury in blowout fracture?", a: "The <b>infraorbital nerve (V2)</b> travels through the <b>infraorbital groove/canal</b> along the orbital floor.<br>When the floor fractures, the nerve is <b>directly contused, stretched, or entrapped</b> in the fracture site → <b>hypoesthesia</b> of the cheek, upper lip, upper teeth, and lateral nose." },
{ q: "A child with orbital trauma has minimal bruising but severe restricted upgaze, nausea, vomiting, and bradycardia. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>White-eyed blowout fracture</b> with <b>inferior rectus entrapment</b>.<br>The minimal external injury (\"white eye\") is deceptive. The <b>oculocardiac reflex</b> from tight muscle entrapment causes bradycardia and vagal symptoms.<br>This is an <b>emergency requiring urgent surgical release</b> within hours." },
{ q: "What causes the deep AC in a posterior open globe wound?", a: "In a <b>posterior scleral wound</b>, <b>vitreous is lost posteriorly</b> through the wound.<br>This reduces posterior segment volume, causing the <b>lens-iris diaphragm to fall back</b>, resulting in a <b>deeper than normal anterior chamber</b>." },
{ q: "What is the CRASH trial and its relevance to traumatic optic neuropathy?", a: "The <b>CRASH trial</b> (Corticosteroid Randomisation After Significant Head Injury) showed that <b>high-dose IV methylprednisolone in head trauma was associated with increased mortality</b>.<br>This has made the use of steroids in <b>traumatic optic neuropathy controversial</b>, and many centers have moved away from routine steroid use." },
{ q: "What is the most common cause of enucleation after penetrating trauma?", a: "<b>Sympathetic ophthalmia prevention</b> has historically been a major reason for enucleation of a severely damaged eye.<br>However, the decision is controversial — modern immunosuppression may control the disease, and enucleation of a seeing (even poorly seeing) eye is rarely justified." },
{ q: "How quickly can sympathetic ophthalmia develop after penetrating injury?", a: "Sympathetic ophthalmia typically develops <b>2 weeks to several months</b> after penetrating injury, with <b>80% occurring within 3 months</b> and <b>90% within 1 year</b>.<br>It can rarely occur as early as <b>5 days</b> or as late as <b>decades</b> after injury." },
{ q: "What is the classic presentation of sympathetic ophthalmia in the sympathizing eye?", a: "The sympathizing (fellow) eye develops:<br>• <b>Bilateral granulomatous panuveitis</b><br>• <b>Mutton-fat KPs</b> (keratic precipitates)<br>• <b>Iris nodules</b> (Koeppe and Busacca)<br>• <b>Vitritis</b><br>• <b>Exudative retinal detachment</b><br>• <b>Dalen-Fuchs nodules</b> (yellow-white lesions at RPE level)<br>• Papillitis (optic disc edema)" },
{ q: "What is the significance of the rosette cataract pattern?", a: "A <b>rosette (flower-petal) cataract</b> is <b>pathognomonic for trauma</b>.<br>• Feathery spoke-like opacities radiating from the lens center<br>• Can involve anterior and/or posterior subcapsular cortex<br>• May develop <b>immediately or years after blunt/penetrating trauma</b><br>Seeing this pattern on exam should prompt a history of ocular trauma." },
{ q: "How does a traumatic retinal detachment from dialysis typically present?", a: "Often presents <b>insidiously, weeks to months after trauma</b>:<br>• Progressive <b>visual field loss</b> (inferior field loss from superior RD)<br>• <b>Floaters</b><br>• <b>Flashes</b> may be absent (unlike PVD-related tears)<br>• Fundoscopy: <b>retinal tear at ora serrata</b> with slowly progressive RD<br>• Most common in <b>young males</b>" },
{ q: "What organisms are most commonly associated with post-traumatic endophthalmitis?", a: "Post-traumatic endophthalmitis organisms:<br>• <b>Gram-positive</b>: <i>Staphylococcus</i> spp., <i>Streptococcus</i> spp., <b><i>Bacillus cereus</i></b> (especially soil-contaminated injuries — fulminant, devastating)<br>• <b>Gram-negative</b>: <i>Pseudomonas</i><br>• Fungi (delayed, soil/vegetable matter exposure)<br><b><i>Bacillus cereus</i></b> has the worst prognosis." },
{ q: "What distinguishes a contusion from a lamellar laceration under BETT?", a: "<b>Contusion</b>: <b>blunt</b> force → no visible wound (internal damage: hyphema, angle recession, commotio retinae, etc.)<br><b>Lamellar laceration</b>: <b>sharp</b> object → <b>partial-thickness wound</b> (the eye wall is cut but not penetrated fully)<br>Both are <b>closed globe</b> injuries." },
{ q: "What is the key differentiating factor between Grade III and Grade IV Roper-Hall chemical burns?", a: "<b>Grade III</b>: corneal haze but <b>iris details may still be partially visible</b>; 1/3–1/2 limbal ischemia → guarded prognosis<br><b>Grade IV</b>: <b>opaque cornea</b> with <b>no iris details visible</b>; > 1/2 limbal ischemia → <b>poor prognosis</b>" },
{ q: "A welder presents with bilateral eye pain, tearing, and photophobia starting 8 hours after work. Diagnosis and management?", a: "<b>Diagnosis</b>: UV keratitis (photokeratitis/welding flash burn).<br>Classic: <b>delayed onset 6–12 hours</b>, bilateral, severe pain.<br><b>Exam</b>: bilateral punctate epithelial erosions with fluorescein staining.<br><b>Management</b>: topical antibiotics, cycloplegic, oral analgesics, reassurance (self-resolving in 24–48 hours)." },
{ q: "What is the typical history that differentiates traumatic from spontaneous subconjunctival hemorrhage?", a: "<b>Traumatic</b>: direct blow or open globe injury → check for open globe signs, especially if 360° or posterior limit not visible.<br><b>Spontaneous</b>: occurs after Valsalva (coughing, sneezing, straining), or with anticoagulant use/HTN.<br>• Spontaneous is usually <b>sectoral, painless, and benign</b>.<br>• If recurrent spontaneous → check <b>BP and coagulation</b>." },
{ q: "Why is timolol preferred for IOP control in hyphema?", a: "<b>Topical timolol</b> (beta-blocker):<br>• Reduces aqueous humor production<br>• Does <b>not affect pupil size</b> (important — don't disturb the clot)<br>• <b>Safe in sickle cell patients</b> (unlike acetazolamide and epinephrine)<br>• Effective IOP reduction without increasing bleeding risk" },
{ q: "What is iridodialysis?", a: "<b>Iridodialysis</b> = <b>detachment of the iris root from the ciliary body</b> at its insertion point, caused by blunt trauma.<br>• Creates a <b>D-shaped pupil</b><br>• May cause <b>monocular diplopia and glare</b><br>• Often associated with hyphema<br>• Can be repaired surgically if symptomatic" },
{ q: "What is cyclodialysis and its significance?", a: "<b>Cyclodialysis</b> = <b>separation of the ciliary body from the scleral spur</b>, creating a direct communication between the anterior chamber and suprachoroidal space.<br>• Results in <b>hypotony</b> (low IOP) — aqueous drains into suprachoroidal space<br>• Seen on gonioscopy as a <b>cyclodialysis cleft</b><br>• May require surgical repair (laser, cryotherapy, or direct suture)" },
{ q: "What are the complications of untreated angle recession?", a: "The primary complication is <b>angle recession glaucoma</b>:<br>• Damaged trabecular meshwork → <b>reduced aqueous outflow</b><br>• <b>Open-angle glaucoma</b> mechanism<br>• May not manifest for <b>months to years</b><br>• Risk correlates with <b>extent of recession</b> (> 180° = higher risk)<br>• Requires <b>lifelong IOP monitoring</b>" },
{ q: "What percentage of angle recession is associated with higher risk of glaucoma?", a: "Angle recession involving <b>> 180° (> 6 clock hours)</b> of the angle is associated with significantly higher risk of developing glaucoma.<br>However, ANY degree of angle recession warrants <b>lifelong monitoring</b>." },
{ q: "A patient presents with monocular diplopia after blunt eye trauma. What should you suspect?", a: "Suspect <b>lens subluxation</b> — partial displacement of the lens from its normal position.<br>• The lens edge bisects the pupil → light passes through both phakic and aphakic zones → <b>monocular diplopia</b><br>• Look for: <b>iridodonesis</b>, <b>phacodonesis</b>, <b>unequal AC depth</b><br>• Also consider: corneal irregularity, iris damage" },
{ q: "What systemic condition is associated with spontaneous lens subluxation (non-traumatic)?", a: "Key associations:<br>• <b>Marfan syndrome</b> (superotemporal subluxation — most common cause)<br>• <b>Homocystinuria</b> (inferonasal subluxation)<br>• <b>Ehlers-Danlos syndrome</b><br>• <b>Weill-Marchesani syndrome</b> (microspherophakia)<br>If lens subluxation is found without trauma → evaluate for these conditions." },
{ q: "What is the role of CT in IOFB evaluation?", a: "<b>CT orbit (non-contrast, thin cuts)</b> is the imaging study of choice for IOFB:<br>• Detects <b>metallic, glass, and stone</b> foreign bodies with high sensitivity<br>• Localizes FB relative to the globe and orbit<br>• Rules out associated fractures<br>• <b>Wood and organic matter</b> may be poorly visualized on CT (may appear similar to air or be iso-dense with soft tissue)<br>• <b>MRI contraindicated if metallic FB suspected</b>" },
{ q: "What is the recommended pH target after chemical eye burn irrigation?", a: "The target pH after irrigation is <b>7.0–7.4</b> (physiologic pH).<br>Check pH with litmus paper in the inferior fornix <b>5–10 minutes after stopping irrigation</b>.<br>If pH has not normalized, <b>resume irrigation</b> and recheck." },
{ q: "What is the mechanism by which citric acid/citrate drops help in chemical burns?", a: "<b>Citric acid (citrate drops)</b>:<br>• Inhibits <b>PMN (polymorphonuclear leukocyte) migration</b> into the cornea<br>• Reduces <b>PMN-mediated collagenolysis</b> (breakdown of corneal collagen by matrix metalloproteinases)<br>• Helps prevent <b>corneal melting and perforation</b><br>• Used as adjunct therapy in the repair phase" },
{ q: "How does amniotic membrane transplant help in severe chemical burns?", a: "<b>Amniotic membrane transplant (AMT)</b>:<br>• Provides a <b>biological scaffold</b> for re-epithelialization<br>• Has <b>anti-inflammatory and anti-scarring</b> properties<br>• Reduces pain<br>• Prevents <b>symblepharon</b> formation<br>• Used in moderate-to-severe burns (Roper-Hall Grade II–IV)" },
{ q: "What is the difference between a penetrating injury with IOFB and a perforating injury?", a: "<b>Penetrating + IOFB</b>: A sharp object enters the eye and <b>leaves a retained foreign body</b> inside the globe (single entry wound + FB inside).<br><b>Perforating</b>: An object enters <b>and exits</b> the eye (two wounds — entry and exit) with <b>no retained FB</b> (it passed through)." },
{ q: "What are the key differences between direct and indirect traumatic optic neuropathy on CT?", a: "<b>Direct TON</b>:<br>• CT may show <b>bone fragment impinging on or lacerating the optic nerve/canal</b><br>• Associated with orbital/facial fractures<br><br><b>Indirect TON</b>:<br>• CT may be <b>normal</b> (no visible fracture through the optic canal)<br>• Damage is from <b>transmitted shear forces</b> (contrecoup)<br>• More common than direct TON" },
{ q: "A patient had penetrating eye injury 3 weeks ago. Now the fellow eye develops pain, photophobia, and blurred vision with mutton-fat KPs. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Sympathetic ophthalmia</b>.<br>• Penetrating trauma to one eye (exciting eye) → autoimmune reaction → <b>bilateral granulomatous panuveitis</b><br>• Fellow eye (sympathizing eye) shows mutton-fat KPs, cells/flare, possible Dalen-Fuchs nodules<br>• Urgent treatment: <b>high-dose systemic steroids + immunosuppression</b>" },
{ q: "What is the most common mechanism of orbital floor fracture in adults?", a: "The <b>hydraulic mechanism</b>: a blunt object (fist, ball) larger than the orbital opening strikes the orbit → <b>sudden increase in intraorbital hydraulic pressure</b> → the weakest wall (floor) fractures outward into the maxillary sinus.<br>An alternative is the <b>buckling mechanism</b>: direct force to the inferior orbital rim transmits to the thin floor." },
{ q: "What instruction should patients with orbital fractures receive regarding nose blowing?", a: "Patients should be strictly instructed to <b>avoid blowing their nose</b> for at least <b>2 weeks</b>.<br>Nose blowing can force <b>air from the sinuses through the fracture into the orbit</b>, causing:<br>• <b>Orbital emphysema</b> (worsening proptosis)<br>• Potential <b>orbital compartment syndrome</b><br>• Increased risk of <b>orbital cellulitis</b> (sinus organisms entering orbit)" },
{ q: "What are the indications for emergent (vs delayed) surgical repair of orbital fractures?", a: "<b>Emergent repair</b> (within hours):<br>• <b>White-eyed blowout in children</b> (muscle entrapment with oculocardiac reflex)<br>• <b>Muscle entrapment</b> with restriction and oculocardiac reflex<br><br><b>Delayed repair</b> (1–2 weeks):<br>• Persistent diplopia in primary gaze after swelling resolves<br>• Enophthalmos > 2 mm<br>• Large floor defect > 50%<br>Most fractures are observed initially as many symptoms resolve with edema." },
{ q: "What medication is given prophylactically to prevent posterior synechiae in traumatic iritis and hyphema?", a: "<b>Topical cycloplegic/mydriatic</b> (e.g., atropine or cyclopentolate).<br>• Dilates the pupil → keeps the iris <b>away from the lens</b><br>• Prevents <b>posterior synechiae</b> (adhesion of iris to anterior lens capsule)<br>• Also provides pain relief by <b>relaxing the ciliary muscle</b> (reduces spasm)" },
{ q: "How does the management of hyphema differ between an adult with no comorbidities and a sickle cell patient?", a: "<b>Standard adult</b>:<br>• Shield, head elevation, atropine, topical steroid, timolol, avoid NSAIDs, daily IOP checks<br>• Acetazolamide can be used for elevated IOP<br><br><b>Sickle cell patient</b>:<br>• Same general measures BUT:<br>• <b>NO acetazolamide</b> (worsens AC sickling)<br>• <b>NO epinephrine</b> (vasoconstriction)<br>• <b>Lower threshold for surgical AC washout</b> (IOP > 24 mmHg for > 24h)" },
{ q: "What is the significance of a positive forced duction test in orbital fracture?", a: "A <b>positive forced duction test</b> = the eye <b>cannot be passively moved</b> in the restricted direction, confirming <b>mechanical entrapment</b> of tissue/muscle.<br>This is distinguished from <b>paresis</b> (negative forced duction — eye moves passively but not actively).<br>Note: <b>Do NOT perform if open globe is suspected.</b>" },
{ q: "What grading of hyphema has the highest risk of corneal blood staining?", a: "<b>Grade IV (total/8-ball hyphema)</b> has the highest risk of corneal blood staining because:<br>• <b>Maximum blood contact with corneal endothelium</b><br>• Often associated with <b>elevated IOP</b><br>• Prolonged contact + elevated IOP drives hemoglobin into stroma" },
{ q: "What is the typical timeline for development of disc pallor after traumatic optic neuropathy?", a: "<b>Optic disc pallor</b> (optic atrophy) develops approximately <b>4–6 weeks</b> after the injury.<br>The fundus may appear <b>completely normal initially</b> despite severe vision loss — this is a key diagnostic challenge.<br>An <b>APD</b> is the critical early sign." },
{ q: "What organisms should be covered by prophylactic antibiotics in open globe IOFB injury?", a: "Must cover:<br>• <b>Gram-positive</b> organisms (most common): <i>Staphylococcus</i>, <i>Streptococcus</i>, <b><i>Bacillus</i></b> → <b>vancomycin</b><br>• <b>Gram-negative</b> organisms: <i>Pseudomonas</i>, <i>Enterobacteriaceae</i> → <b>ceftazidime or fluoroquinolone</b><br>IOFB injuries carry a <b>higher endophthalmitis risk</b> (up to 7–13%) than simple lacerations." },
{ q: "Alkali burns are most commonly caused by which household/industrial agents?", a: "Most common alkali agents:<br>1. <b>Ammonia (NH₃)</b> — cleaning products, fertilizers (penetrates rapidly)<br>2. <b>Sodium hydroxide (NaOH/lye)</b> — drain cleaners, oven cleaners<br>3. <b>Calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)₂/lime)</b> — cement, plaster, whitewash<br>4. <b>Potassium hydroxide (KOH)</b> — some cleaning agents<br>Ammonia penetrates the fastest; lime particles can lodge in fornices." },
{ q: "Why must lime particles be manually removed from the conjunctival fornices after exposure?", a: "<b>Lime (calcium hydroxide)</b> particles can lodge in the conjunctival fornices and <b>continue to release alkali</b> even during irrigation, acting as a <b>reservoir of ongoing chemical injury</b>.<br>Must <b>evert the eyelids and sweep the fornices</b> to remove all particulate matter before irrigation will be effective." },
{ q: "How do you differentiate traumatic mydriasis from pharmacologic dilation?", a: "<b>Traumatic mydriasis</b>:<br>• History of blunt trauma<br>• <b>Irregular pupil</b> (visible sphincter tears on slit-lamp)<br>• May have associated hyphema, iritis, angle recession<br>• Pupil does <b>not constrict</b> with pilocarpine (mechanical sphincter damage)<br><br><b>Pharmacologic dilation</b>:<br>• <b>Round, fully dilated pupil</b><br>• Does not constrict with pilocarpine (if anticholinergic agent)<br>• No trauma signs" },
{ q: "What is the significance of hypotony in the setting of ocular trauma?", a: "<b>Hypotony</b> (IOP < 5 mmHg) in trauma suggests:<br>1. <b>Open globe injury</b> — aqueous leaking through wound (most important to rule out)<br>2. <b>Cyclodialysis cleft</b> — aqueous draining into suprachoroidal space<br>3. <b>Ciliary body shutdown</b> (traumatic ciliary body inflammation reducing aqueous production)<br>Must rule out open globe before attributing to other causes." },
{ q: "What is the role of B-scan ultrasonography in ocular trauma?", a: "<b>B-scan ultrasonography</b> is used when the <b>posterior segment cannot be visualized</b> (e.g., dense vitreous hemorrhage, cataract, hyphema).<br>Can identify:<br>• <b>Retinal detachment</b><br>• <b>Vitreous hemorrhage</b><br>• <b>IOFB</b><br>• <b>Choroidal detachment</b><br><b>Caution</b>: avoid excessive pressure on the globe if open globe is suspected — use very light contact or immersion technique." },
{ q: "A construction worker is hit in the eye with a nail gun. What type of open globe injury classification applies?", a: "This is an <b>open globe laceration</b>.<br>Subtype depends on whether:<br>• Nail enters but does not exit → <b>IOFB</b> (if nail remains inside) or <b>penetrating</b> (if removed)<br>• Nail enters and exits → <b>perforating</b> injury<br>Management: shield, NPO, CT orbit, IV antibiotics, emergent surgical repair." },
{ q: "What finding on gonioscopy differentiates angle recession from a normal wide angle?", a: "<b>Asymmetry between the two eyes</b> is the key differentiator.<br>In angle recession:<br>• The <b>ciliary body band is abnormally widened</b> in the injured eye compared to the fellow eye<br>• The <b>iris insertion is more posterior</b><br>• A <b>torn iris process</b> may be seen<br>Normal wide angles (e.g., in myopes) are <b>symmetric bilaterally</b>." },
{ q: "What is the standard initial management sequence for any patient with ocular trauma?", a: "1. <b>Visual acuity</b> (document FIRST — most important prognostic indicator)<br>2. <b>Pupil exam</b> (check for APD)<br>3. <b>External exam</b> (lids, adnexa, orbit)<br>4. If open globe suspected → <b>STOP exam</b>, apply rigid shield<br>5. <b>CT orbit</b> (if FB or fracture suspected)<br>6. <b>Slit-lamp exam</b> (if globe integrity confirmed)<br>7. <b>Dilated fundus exam</b> (if safe)<br>8. <b>IOP measurement</b> (only if globe integrity confirmed)" },
{ q: "What complications can arise from a retained organic IOFB (e.g., wood)?", a: "<b>Organic IOFBs</b> (wood, vegetable matter) carry high risk of:<br>• <b>Endophthalmitis</b> (especially fungal — <i>Aspergillus</i>, <i>Fusarium</i>)<br>• <b>Abscess formation</b><br>• <b>Intense inflammatory reaction</b><br>• Difficult to visualize on CT (may be iso-dense with soft tissue)<br>• <b>MRI may be helpful</b> for non-metallic organic material (only if metallic FB excluded first)" },
{ q: "What is the difference between an exciting eye and a sympathizing eye?", a: "<b>Exciting eye</b> = the eye that sustained the <b>original penetrating trauma</b> or surgery (the injured eye).<br><b>Sympathizing eye</b> = the <b>fellow (uninvolved) eye</b> that develops sympathetic ophthalmia.<br>The autoimmune reaction targets <b>uveal antigens</b> exposed by the original injury." },
{ q: "What are Koeppe and Busacca nodules?", a: "<b>Koeppe nodules</b> = small nodules at the <b>pupillary margin</b> of the iris.<br><b>Busacca nodules</b> = nodules on the <b>iris stroma</b> (surface of the iris, away from the pupil margin).<br>Both are found in <b>granulomatous uveitis</b> (e.g., sympathetic ophthalmia, sarcoidosis, TB, VKH)." },
{ q: "What are the components of a dilated fundus exam that are important after blunt trauma?", a: "After blunt trauma, look for:<br>1. <b>Commotio retinae</b> (Berlin edema — retinal whitening)<br>2. <b>Choroidal rupture</b> (crescent-shaped streak)<br>3. <b>Retinal dialysis/tears</b> (especially inferotemporal)<br>4. <b>Vitreous hemorrhage</b><br>5. <b>Retinal detachment</b><br>6. <b>Optic nerve pallor</b> (if chronic)<br>7. <b>Macular hole</b> (traumatic)<br>8. <b>Subretinal hemorrhage</b>" },
{ q: "What type of uveitis does sympathetic ophthalmia cause?", a: "<b>Bilateral diffuse granulomatous panuveitis</b>.<br>• <b>Pan</b> = involves all layers (anterior, intermediate, and posterior uvea)<br>• <b>Granulomatous</b> = mutton-fat KPs, iris nodules, Dalen-Fuchs nodules<br>• Involves <b>both eyes</b> (exciting and sympathizing)" },
{ q: "Name three conditions that can mimic sympathetic ophthalmia.", a: "Conditions that can present similarly:<br>1. <b>Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH) syndrome</b> — bilateral granulomatous panuveitis, but NO history of penetrating trauma<br>2. <b>Sarcoidosis</b> — bilateral granulomatous uveitis, systemic findings<br>3. <b>Phacoantigenic (lens-induced) uveitis</b> — granulomatous reaction to lens material, typically unilateral" },
{ q: "What factors increase the risk of endophthalmitis after open globe injury?", a: "Risk factors for post-traumatic endophthalmitis:<br>1. <b>Retained IOFB</b> (highest risk factor)<br>2. <b>Delay in surgical repair</b> (> 24 hours)<br>3. <b>Rural/agricultural setting</b> (organic contamination)<br>4. <b>Lens capsule disruption</b><br>5. <b>Posterior wound location</b><br>6. <b>Dirty/contaminated injury</b><br>Prophylactic IV antibiotics significantly reduce this risk." },
{ q: "What is a traumatic macular hole and how does it present?", a: "A <b>traumatic macular hole</b> is a full-thickness defect in the fovea from blunt trauma.<br>• Presents with <b>central scotoma</b> and decreased central visual acuity<br>• Fundoscopy: round, well-defined hole in the macula<br>• <b>OCT</b> confirms full-thickness defect<br>• Some may close spontaneously; others require <b>vitrectomy with gas tamponade</b>" },
{ q: "What class of medication should be avoided in ALL hyphema patients (regardless of sickle cell status)?", a: "<b>Aspirin and NSAIDs</b> must be avoided in ALL hyphema patients because:<br>• They inhibit <b>platelet aggregation</b><br>• Increase the risk of <b>rebleeding</b> (which is most dangerous on days 3–5)<br>• Acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic" },
{ q: "What is the Fox shield?", a: "A <b>Fox shield</b> is a <b>rigid, protective eye shield</b> (metal or clear plastic) that is:<br>• Placed over the orbit (resting on the brow ridge and cheek/maxilla)<br>• Taped (not tied) to the face<br>• Does <b>NOT touch or apply pressure to the globe</b><br>• Used in <b>open globe injuries</b> to prevent further extrusion of intraocular contents<br>It is the standard of care for globe protection in ocular trauma." },
{ q: "What is the prognosis of commotio retinae (Berlin edema)?", a: "<b>Commotio retinae usually resolves spontaneously</b> over weeks.<br>• If <b>peripheral</b> → generally excellent visual prognosis<br>• If involving the <b>macula</b> → may cause permanent RPE changes and photoreceptor damage → <b>reduced central vision</b><br>No specific treatment; observation." },
{ q: "How does choroidal neovascularization (CNVM) relate to traumatic choroidal rupture?", a: "<b>CNVM</b> can develop as a <b>late complication</b> of choroidal rupture, especially if the rupture passes through the macula.<br>• New blood vessels grow from the choroid through Bruch's membrane defect<br>• Can cause <b>subretinal hemorrhage, fluid, and vision loss</b><br>• Treatment: <b>anti-VEGF injections</b> (bevacizumab, ranibizumab)" },
{ q: "What is the appropriate initial irrigation solution for chemical eye burns if normal saline is unavailable?", a: "<b>Any clean water</b> (tap water, bottled water) should be used <b>immediately</b>.<br>The key principle is: <b>do not delay irrigation searching for an ideal solution</b>.<br>Preferred solutions (in order): <b>balanced salt solution > Ringer's lactate > normal saline > tap water</b>.<br>Even <b>tap water</b> is appropriate as the osmotic effect is negligible compared to the chemical damage." },
{ q: "What is the relationship between the extent of limbal ischemia and prognosis in chemical burns?", a: "Greater limbal ischemia → <b>worse prognosis</b>:<br>• Limbal ischemia reflects <b>destruction of limbal blood supply and stem cells</b><br>• < 1/3 limbal ischemia (Grade II) → good prognosis<br>• 1/3–1/2 (Grade III) → guarded<br>• > 1/2 (Grade IV) → <b>poor prognosis</b> (likely to need stem cell transplant and corneal transplant)" },
];
